 # 2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization
 
-Also called: EWA Projection, Elliptical Weighted Average projection, 2D Gaussian Projection, Gaussian Projection, Linearized 2D Gaussian Projection
+Also called: 2D Gaussian Projection, 3D Gaussian Projection, 3D to 2D Projection in Gaussian Splatting, EWA Projection, Elliptical Weighted Average projection, Gaussian Projection, Linearized 2D Gaussian Projection, Splat Projection
 
 ## Overview
-2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization is the core rasterization primitive of 3D Gaussian Splatting that maps an anisotropic 3D Gaussian volume primitive into a 2D elliptical footprint (splat) on the image plane [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering millions of 3D probability distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in screen space [S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Optimal Gaussian Splatting Projection](3311-optimal-gaussian-splatting-projection.md) by using a fixed image-plane projection rather than per-Gaussian tangent planes, and from [Render Means](2070-render-means.md) by explicitly computing the screen-space covariance matrix to handle shape and spread [S1, unofficial]. The method stabilizes the projection by adding a small epsilon to the diagonal of the 2D covariance matrix to guarantee positive definiteness and prevent aliasing [S2, unofficial].
+2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization is the core rasterization primitive of 3D Gaussian Splatting that maps an anisotropic 3D Gaussian volume primitive into a 2D elliptical footprint (splat) on the image plane [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering millions of 3D probability distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in screen space [S3, unofficial]. It explicitly computes the screen-space covariance matrix to handle shape and spread, unlike point-sprite methods [S1, unofficial]. The method stabilizes the projection by adding a small epsilon to the diagonal of the 2D covariance matrix to guarantee positive definiteness and numerical stability [S2, unofficial].
 
